  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 226 PM EDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nSurface and upper ridge for Thursday and Friday producing \nunseasonably warm and dry conditions. Temperatures will be near 10 \ndegrees above normal.\n\nMain weather message is the developing Tropical Storm Isaias which \nis forecast become a Hurricane before making landfall across the \ncentral Gulf Coast sometime Friday evening. This storm will move \nquickly north due phasing with an upper trough moving north as well. \nLatest Tropical deterministic and ensemble models are showing path \nforecast converging with a northward movement of the surface low \ninto west/middle Tennessee for Saturday. The storm moves quickly \nnortheast into the eastern Ohio valley for Sunday.\n\nGiven the expected path the expected impacts are as follow for \nSaturday through Sunday morning:\n\n1. Locally heavy rainfall possible beginning Saturday for mainly the \nPlateau, southeast Tennessee, and southwest North Carolina. \nDownslope winds into the northern and central Tennessee valley will \nlimit the QPF elsewhere.\n\n2. Developing high winds for Saturday. Depending on how fast Isaias \nmoves north could begin before daybreak across southern Plateau and \nsoutheast Tennessee. Due to the pressure gradient from surface \nwedging over the Carolinas and Isaias, mountain wave high winds look \nlikely for the far east Tennessee mountains and foothills possibly \nin excess of 70 mph.\n\n3. For the rest of east Tennessee, southwest Virginia and southwest \nNorth Carolina, winds of 15 to 30 mph with gusts up to 45 mph are \npossible. Higher gusts over the southern Plateau and southeast \nTennessee counties are likely. Higher elevations around the \nChattanooga area could see gusts by daybreak Saturday in excess of \n60 mph, especially Lookout and Signal mountains. \n\nFor Saturday, instability is quite limited and elevated so severe \nstorms are not anticipated.\n\nFor Sunday, remnants of Isaias moves north and northeast of the \nregion with chance of showers continuing. \n\nFor Monday through Wednesday, upper and surface ridging builds into \nthe region with return of sunshine and drier conditions.",
